      "role": "user",
      "content": "\"process entropy\" is a good term, i'll use that. it actually describes it perfectly.\n\nbut honestly, it's getting harder to be that clinical when the behavior itself feels so... small and specific? it's like they're almost invisible most of the time, just waiting for a moment of high pressure to drop one tiny, annoying thing into the gears that trips everyone up. it feels less like a \"process deviation\" and more like there's this little, persistent entity just... existing to mess with the system.\n\nif i'm sitting in that HR meeting and i want to explain that this person's whole *presence* feels like a tiny, disruptive force that just creates these small, unseen problems, how do i say that? how do i describe that specific kind of \"hidden, small-scale chaos\" without sounding like i'm talking about a character from a storybook?",
      "source": "evaluator"
