    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the action is too small to be a clear deviation, you must focus on the *effect* on the team's cognitive load and momentum. You are describing a drain on focus, which is a very real, measurable resource in a tech environment. You can describe this \"hidden chaos\" by discussing the *cost of vigilance* it imposes on the rest of the team.\n\nInstead of describing the person as an entity, describe the *state* they induce in the workflow. You could say something like, \"The team is consistently expending an unbudgeted amount of mental energy monitoring for potential small errors, which prevents us from achieving deep focus on high-priority deliverables.\" This frames the issue as a drain on collective capacity, not just a personal failing.\n\nThis is the difference between reporting a misplaced tool (a single error) and reporting that the team has to stop every hour to check if the tool is still where it should be (constant vigilance). That constant checking is the chaos you are describing.",
